              "children": [
                {
                  "id": "lede",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "When you're choosing the right trailer for your operation, dry van trailer weight capacity is one of the most important specifications to understand. For owner-operators and fleet managers, the wrong capacity can cost time, fuel efficiency, and ultimately revenue. A trailer that's too light limits what you can haul. One that's too heavy may reduce payload potential and increase operating costs.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full",
                    "anchor": "post-lede"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-intro-1",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Understanding the basics of dry van sizes, semi-trailer capacity, and utility dry van weight helps you choose equipment that keeps your trucks productive and profitable. At Sun State Trailers, our goal is simple: help you find the equipment that works for your business today and holds its value long-term.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h-what-is",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "What Is Dry Van Trailer Weight Capacity?",
                    "headingLevel": 2,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "what-is"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-what-is-1",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Weight capacity refers to the maximum amount of cargo a trailer can safely carry. To calculate it, you subtract the trailer's empty weight from its gross vehicle weight rating (GVWR).",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-what-is-2",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "<strong>Typical numbers look like this:</strong>",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "checks-what-is-3",
                  "type": "check-list",
                  "props": {
                    "items": [
                      {
                        "text": "~65,000 lbs"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "13,000–16,000 lbs"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "45,000–52,000 lbs"
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-what-is-4",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "This payload capacity determines how much freight you can legally and safely haul. Many operators pay close attention to utility dry van weight because even a small difference in trailer weight can increase or decrease payload capacity.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "co-what-is-5-row",
                  "type": "row",
                  "props": {
                    "gap": 0
                  },
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "id": "co-what-is-5",
                      "type": "column",
                      "props": {
                        "span": 12
                      },
